       t="When you breathe cold air or your skin gets cold, the blood vessels throughout the body narrow. That can raise blood pressure, and it can also narrow the coronary arteries, the vessels that feed the heart. So the heart is working harder while its own blood supply is tighter. For many people this is no problem. For someone with narrowed arteries or high blood pressure, it can be enough to trigger symptoms. Studies have also linked colder periods with more heart problems, but those studies show a link, not proof that cold alone is the cause.",
